WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.040
Speaker 1: Folks, we have to now pray at the altar&nbsp;
of Joe Biden. Joe Biden has now reduced gas prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:17.200
22 days in a row. How did he do it? Obviously&nbsp;
because Joe Biden was to blame for higher gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:26.240
prices in every country. We must now praise Joe&nbsp;
Biden for this notable extended decline to gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.440
prices. I'm not actually being serious, but let me&nbsp;
tell you what it is that is going on and then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:40.560
will talk about it. Gas prices continue to drop.&nbsp;
Gas prices fell another $0.02 yesterday to 477&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:47.600
per gallon, according to the latest data. Down&nbsp;
$0.24 from the highest ever recorded level of $5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.840
and one and a half cents last month. Gas prices&nbsp;
are on track to continue falling, though maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:58.720
not fast enough for President Joe Biden, who&nbsp;
has criticized gas stations for keeping prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:08.400
keeping prices high. So I tweeted sort of tongue&nbsp;
in cheek yesterday. Gas prices have declined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:17.040
21 days in a row. How did Joe Biden do this? It&nbsp;
was completely satirical and with 50,000 likes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:24.000
on that tweet as of this morning and maybe more&nbsp;
at this point in time. Most people understood it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.880
but not everybody. And some people thought I was&nbsp;
being serious and actually responded to me saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.280
David, Joe Biden's irresponsible actions are&nbsp;
responsible for gas prices having gone up. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:44.560
he didn't do anything to lower them. Oh, okay. So&nbsp;
when a Democrat sees higher gas prices, it's their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.800
fault. But when a Democrat sees declining gas&nbsp;
prices, it has nothing to do with that Democrat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.680
I've got it. Now, of course, you would probably be&nbsp;
asking one of the same questions I've been asking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:06.480
How is Joe Biden responsible for gas prices being&nbsp;
up in most countries over the last year? And how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.640
can we now say he has nothing to do if that is his&nbsp;
major responsibility, which is incredible. One man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.800
responsible for higher gas prices globally. Now&nbsp;
they go down and we say it has nothing to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.920
him. That doesn't really make a lot of sense when&nbsp;
gas prices go down under, a Democrat conservative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.560
say nothing. It's only when they go up. And&nbsp;
remember when prices were up under Donald Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.480
conservatives said nothing. They didn't complain.&nbsp;
I wonder why that is. So we can look at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.360
of the data here. GasBuddy is a good website&nbsp;
tracking this. As you can see, there was this peak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:44.160
just under 502 per gallon, right around what&nbsp;
was that, mid-June, I guess we would call it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:51.360
And now gas prices down, not a crazy amount,&nbsp;
down about 5%. Now, there is a couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.640
different things that are going on. One&nbsp;
of them is that the price of crude oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.880
has started to decline relatively significantly.&nbsp;
You might remember that briefly in March,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:10.240
crude oil prices hit. What was this, $123 per&nbsp;
barrel? Crude oil is the source material for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:16.320
what is eventually refined into gasoline that&nbsp;
we. Not me. I'm on electric now but that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.320
put in they're put in their cars. And speaking of&nbsp;
electric vehicles, there's a reason I bring that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:29.600
up. Sustained high gas prices aren't particularly&nbsp;
good for the oil industry. You might think, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:36.000
the higher the gas prices are, the more money they&nbsp;
make in the short and medium term, maybe. But if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.280
the longer the gas prices are sustained at a high&nbsp;
level, the more people will say when it comes time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.880
to replace their vehicle, I'm going to get off&nbsp;
gas altogether and go to an electric vehicle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:50.640
That's not good for the gas for the gasoline&nbsp;
industry. So we add that spike to about $123&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:57.520
per barrel in March. And then we had another&nbsp;
spike almost to the same level, $122 per barrel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:04.000
about a month ago. And now, as of yesterday,&nbsp;
oil prices were all the way down to what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:11.520
this look like about 98, $99 per barrel? That's&nbsp;
a pretty significant decline. In a perfect world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:19.600
you would eventually see that same proportional&nbsp;
decline, about 17, 18% trickle down to gasoline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.880
It almost never happens. I'm reminded of&nbsp;
we've seen those who those of us who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.040
paid attention to or purchased&nbsp;
airline tickets over the last 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.800
20 years, remember that every time that&nbsp;
jet fuel prices and crude oil prices go up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.400
airplane tickets go up, and sometimes there's&nbsp;
fuel surcharges and all these different things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.280
But then when the prices of jet fuel come down and&nbsp;
crude oil come down, those are rarely, if ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.920
removed. They're sort of like permanent. And&nbsp;
we often don't see gas prices. Gas prices will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:58.880
mirror the increases in oil prices, but they will&nbsp;
not mirror the decreases. But needless to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.520
I don't think Joe Biden really has anything&nbsp;
to do with this. I didn't think Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.280
had anything to do with gas prices going up.&nbsp;
And I think he has essentially nothing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.440
with gas prices going down. He released a little&nbsp;
bit of oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.600
Very modest impact. And that that's really the&nbsp;
tool other than, you know, going to war with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:24.640
oil rich nation or a gas tax holiday. Releasing&nbsp;
supply from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.680
one of the very few tools presidents have. This&nbsp;
stuff is global. This stuff is cyclical. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.400
tends to move relatively slowly. We're now it's&nbsp;
about three and a half weeks and it's like a 5%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:41.040
decrease that we've seen. So my expectation and&nbsp;
I don't really make predictions that I would ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:47.840
suggest anyone make any financial decisions on my&nbsp;
expectation. Just based on what we've been seeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.800
we've seen a slowdown in the growth through the&nbsp;
inflation rate. I expect to see that continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.800
to come down. Gas prices are going to be a part&nbsp;
of that. And my my guess is and it's not a gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.760
I'm betting on. Remember, this is my guess. But in&nbsp;
terms of financial stuff, I'm just doing the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:10.080
thing I always do, just every month maintaining&nbsp;
my asset allocation. I'm not making any shorts or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.840
investments in any commodities. I think over the&nbsp;
next six months we will see inflation taper off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.120
We will see gas prices continue to come down,&nbsp;
particularly as we are now. We are now at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.880
time that gas prices are often really expensive.&nbsp;
The summer driving season, as it's often called,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.920
